#:script

###
# Variables in Kobo
Kobo provides a consistent and explicit approach to variable declaration, emphasizing readability, type safety, 
and clarity of intent. Variables are declared using the `var` keyword, followed by an optional type annotation 
separated by a colon, such as `var:int age = 30;` for an explicitly typed integer or `var name = "John";` for an 
inferred type. Kobo supports local variables, instance and static members, function parameters, and global properties, 
each with defined scope and lifetime. Variables can represent primitive types, strings, objects, collections, tuples, unions, 
functions, and user-defined types. Read-only declarations support values that cannot be reassigned, while global properties provide 
controlled access to shared values. This consistent nomenclature and flexible typing model make Kobo code straightforward for both 
human developers and AI coding agents to write, understand, and maintain.




## The basics - simple variable assignment

Note that in kobo variables must be assigned at the point when they are declared.
kobo supports all the same types as c# with the same naming convention for them
###


//uncomment this to see the error
//var:bool b

//boolean
var:bool var1 = true

//integer
var:byte var2 = 30
var:sbyte var3 = 30
var:short var4 = 30
var:ushort var5 = 30
var:int var6 = 30
var:uint var7 = 30
var:long var8 = 30
var:ulong var9 = 30
var:nint var10 = 30
var:nuint var11 = 30

//floating point
var:float var12 = 30.0f 
var:double var13 = 30.0
var:decimal var14 = 30.0M

//string
var:char var15 = '3'
var:string var16 = "30"

//object
var:object var17 = 10
var:dynamic var18 = "something"


###
You'll notice a few new things with this language.  Firstly, there is no need for a semi-colon.
You can include it in your code if you still feel uncomfortable not having it, but we can get along
without you specifying it.  You'll also note a common pattern for declaration (and you'll see it more as
we get further into the language).  To declare a variable you follow the format:
var:*[type name]* *[variable name]* = _value_. 
> Note that there should be no spaces between var:*typename*.  

Type inference can work by removing the type name portion of the declartion (along with the colon)
###

var var19 = 10

###
To quickly print results out you can use the _Print_ function.  Print emulates ```Console.WriteLine``` so it 
can be used anywhere that method is used.
###

//writing to the console
Print("This is writing to the console")

###
Kobo supports arrays and lists as well.  By default, defined collections of values will be treated as lists
if not explicitly declared so the following code produces a ```List<string>``` named planets_list
###

//list of strings
var planets_list = ["my","very","educated","mother","just","served","us","nine","pickles"]

###
While the code below will explicity create an array of strings
###

//arrays
var:string[] planets_array = ["my","very","educated","mother","just","served","us","nine","pickles"]


//list of strings
var inferred_planets_list = ["my","very","educated","mother","just","served","us","nine","pickles"]


###
Kobo also supports dictionary initializers.  Here the special type alias ```dictionary```, not to be confused with ```Dictionary``` is used. Both
create the same dictionary instance. The value this is being set to is a *dictionary literal* and can be used anywhere in a kobo program where a
dictionary is requires.  Kobo will also infer dictionary types based on it.
###

//dictionary literals
var:dictionary<string,int> ages = 
{
    "joe":5,
    "smith":9
}

//type inference in dictionary literals
var ages_2 = 
{
    "david":5,
    "john":9,

}

###

#### 
## Other kinds of things
---
We can expand this pattern a bit further to *[kind]*:*[type]* *[name]* *[definition]*
using this general principle, *constants* are defines as follows:
###

//constants
const:int CONSTANT = 100


###
We previously showed writing out to the console with the Print command.  Kobo also has an ```Input``` command for 
taking input from the console.
###


//reading values from the console
var:string input = Input()
Print(input)

###
```Input``` can take also accept an argument.  When this is passed in it will be presented to the user as a prompt.  
###

//prompting the user as part of reading values from the console
var:string input2 = Input("enter some text: ")
Print(input2)




Print("Sample complete!")

